1
00:00:00,773 --> 00:00:04,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Today's sutta is the Majjhima Nikāya Sālekha Sutta.

2
00:00:05,895 --> 00:00:10,378
[SPEAKER_00]: So, Majjhima Nikāya Eighth Sālekha Sutta, as we translated it, wearing away.

3
00:00:11,398 --> 00:00:13,199
[SPEAKER_00]: So, thus have I heard.

4
00:00:13,479 --> 00:00:19,002
[SPEAKER_00]: At one time, the auspicious one was staying near Savatthi in Jeta's Grove, Anathapindika's monastery.

5
00:00:19,862 --> 00:00:24,544
[SPEAKER_00]: In the late afternoon, Venerable Mahācunda came out of retreat and went to the auspicious one.

6
00:00:25,565 --> 00:00:28,106
[SPEAKER_00]: He bowed and sat to one side and said to the auspicious one,

7
00:00:29,716 --> 00:00:38,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Said to the auspicious one, eh... venerable sir, there are many different views that arise in the world, connected with the assertions about the self or the world.

8
00:00:38,768 --> 00:00:44,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Does the abandoning and relinquishing of those views come about in a bhikkhu who attends merely to the fundamentals of the training?

9
00:00:47,192 --> 00:00:49,436
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha repeats Chunda.

10
00:00:49,536 --> 00:00:56,948
[SPEAKER_00]: There are many different views that arise in the world, connected with assertions about the self or the world.

11
00:00:56,968 --> 00:01:01,575
[SPEAKER_00]: Abhikūr relinquishes and abandons these views by seeing as it is with right understanding.

12
00:01:06,601 --> 00:01:13,746
[SPEAKER_00]: Where they arise, where they settle, and where they operate, as this is not mine, I'm not this, this is not myself.

13
00:01:14,747 --> 00:01:18,170
[SPEAKER_00]: So, full understanding of the views is the way to relinquish them.

14
00:01:19,591 --> 00:01:34,983
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, the Buddha goes on and says, it is possible that a certain bhikkhu, having thoroughly withdrawn himself from sensuality, having withdrawn from unbeneficial phenomena, with thinking and with pondering, with joy and ease born of that withdrawal, might abide having entered upon the first jhāna.

15
00:01:36,805 --> 00:01:41,955
[SPEAKER_00]: He might think he's practicing wearing away, as in, like, wearing away of defilements.

16
00:01:43,430 --> 00:01:48,251
[SPEAKER_00]: But, in the training of the Noble Ones, these are not called wearing away, i.e.

17
00:01:48,311 --> 00:01:53,693
[SPEAKER_00]: the first jhāna is not called wearing away, it's called easeful abiding in the present experience.

18
00:01:54,353 --> 00:02:06,216
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, he goes on to say, it is possible that Sambhikhu, with the appeasement of thinking and pondering, with internal confidence and collectedness of mind, without thinking and pondering... wait, what?

19
00:02:07,136 --> 00:02:09,697
[SPEAKER_00]: It is possible that Sambhikhu, with the appeasement of thinking and pondering... aha!

20
00:02:10,498 --> 00:02:19,122
[SPEAKER_00]: With the appeasement of thinking and pondering, with internal confidence and correctness of mind, without thinking and pondering, with joy and ease born of composure, might enter upon the second jhāna.

21
00:02:19,722 --> 00:02:24,644
[SPEAKER_00]: So, with appeasement of thinking and pondering, the thinking and pondering is without... he's without.

22
00:02:25,444 --> 00:02:30,866
[SPEAKER_00]: And he can then experience joy and ease born of that composure and abide in the second jhāna.

23
00:02:32,287 --> 00:02:39,550
[SPEAKER_00]: He might think he's practicing wearing away, but in the training of the Noble Ones, these are not called wearing away, they're called easeful abidings with the present experience.

24
00:02:41,166 --> 00:02:42,627
[SPEAKER_00]: Same goes for the third jhāna.

25
00:02:44,528 --> 00:02:46,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Same goes for the fourth jhāna.

26
00:02:46,809 --> 00:02:47,809
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to read it all.

27
00:02:49,010 --> 00:02:50,271
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he goes even further.

28
00:02:50,291 --> 00:03:06,079
[SPEAKER_00]: He says that it is possible that Saṃbhīku, having fully surmounted perceptions of form, with the ending of perceptions of resistance, not attending to the perceptions of multiplicity, knows that space is infinite, might abide having entered upon the extent of that infinite space.

29
00:03:06,379 --> 00:03:08,460
[SPEAKER_00]: He might think this is practicing wearing away,

30
00:03:09,895 --> 00:03:14,417
[SPEAKER_00]: But in the training of the noble ones, these are not called wearing away, they are called peaceful abidings.

31
00:03:16,919 --> 00:03:28,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Goes further into the fully surmounting the extent of infinite space, knowing that consciousness is infinite, abiding upon the extent of infinite consciousness, but that too is not wearing away.

32
00:03:30,006 --> 00:03:38,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, having fully surmounted the extent of infinite consciousness, knowing that there is nothing, might abide having entered upon the extent of that nothingness.

33
00:03:40,515 --> 00:03:42,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, that too is not wearing away.

34
00:03:43,257 --> 00:03:44,618
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a peaceful abiding.

35
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:55,767
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it is possible that Saṃbhiko, having fully surmounted the extent of nothingness, might abide entering upon the extent of neither perception nor non-perception.

36
00:03:56,528 --> 00:04:01,192
[SPEAKER_00]: And he might think he's practicing wearing away, but in the training of the Noble Ones, these are not called wearing away.

37
00:04:01,492 --> 00:04:02,913
[SPEAKER_00]: They're called peaceful abidings.

38
00:04:04,955 --> 00:04:07,457
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, the Buddha goes to explain what actually is

39
00:04:08,896 --> 00:04:10,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Wearing away of the defilements.

40
00:04:12,837 --> 00:04:16,738
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as it is here, Chandra, that wearing away should be practiced like this.

41
00:04:17,158 --> 00:04:20,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will be cruel, but here we will not be cruel.

42
00:04:21,359 --> 00:04:25,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will kill living creatures, but we will not kill living creatures.

43
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:28,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will steal, but we will not steal.

44
00:04:29,141 --> 00:04:30,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will not be celibate,

45
00:04:34,330 --> 00:04:37,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will not be celibate, but we will be celibate.

46
00:04:37,413 --> 00:04:39,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will lie, we won't lie.

47
00:04:39,756 --> 00:04:44,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will speak divisively, speak harshly, engage in frivolous talk.

48
00:04:44,661 --> 00:04:49,206
[SPEAKER_00]: We won't speak divisively, speak harshly, or engage in frivolous talk.

49
00:04:49,226 --> 00:04:51,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will be covetous, with ill will.

50
00:04:52,530 --> 00:04:55,052
[SPEAKER_00]: We won't be covetous, and we will not have ill will.

51
00:04:55,916 --> 00:04:56,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will have wrong view.

52
00:04:57,316 --> 00:04:58,077
[SPEAKER_00]: We'll have the right view.

53
00:04:58,437 --> 00:05:05,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Others have wrong intention, wrong speech, wrong action, wrong livelihood, wrong effort, wrong recollectedness, wrong composure, wrong knowledge.

54
00:05:06,439 --> 00:05:11,721
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the wrong, the non-noble, hateful part.

55
00:05:12,401 --> 00:05:13,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will have wrong liberation.

56
00:05:13,602 --> 00:05:14,702
[SPEAKER_00]: We will have the right liberation.

57
00:05:14,802 --> 00:05:17,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will be overcome with indolence and lethargy.

58
00:05:17,783 --> 00:05:20,004
[SPEAKER_00]: We will be rid of indolence and lethargy.

59
00:05:20,894 --> 00:05:23,155
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will be restless, we will not be restless.

60
00:05:23,415 --> 00:05:34,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will have doubts, be irritable, be resentful, will denigrate, be contentious, be jealous, be stingy, be devious, deceitful, obstinate, arrogant.

61
00:05:36,441 --> 00:05:37,301
[SPEAKER_00]: We won't be any of that.

62
00:05:38,281 --> 00:05:41,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will be hard to admonish, but we will not be hard to admonish.

63
00:05:42,859 --> 00:05:45,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will have bad friends, we will have good friends.

64
00:05:45,741 --> 00:05:47,621
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will be negligent, we will be diligent.

65
00:05:48,382 --> 00:05:50,243
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will be faithless, we will have faith.

66
00:05:50,503 --> 00:05:53,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will be shameless, we will be ashamed of wrongdoing.

67
00:05:54,725 --> 00:05:56,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will be imprudent, we will be prudent.

68
00:05:57,326 --> 00:05:58,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Unlearned, we will be learned.

69
00:05:58,987 --> 00:06:00,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Lazy, we will be effortful.

70
00:06:01,589 --> 00:06:05,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will have muddled recollectedness, we will have recollectedness properly established.

71
00:06:06,728 --> 00:06:09,750
[SPEAKER_00]: So, before I go further, let's just go through this part.

72
00:06:09,810 --> 00:06:13,012
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as you can see, like, jhānas are not the wearing away, and instead of these kind of

73
00:06:36,847 --> 00:06:42,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, almost like mundane things, you know, like everybody knows, okay, we shouldn't be cruel, we shouldn't lie still.

74
00:06:43,152 --> 00:06:44,973
[SPEAKER_00]: How is that wearing away?

75
00:06:45,834 --> 00:06:58,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Because surely enough, you know, if you look at the just, you know, the vast majority of Buddhist instructions today, the practice is, you know, the meditation, the jhāna and the meditative experiences.

76
00:06:59,745 --> 00:07:05,810
[SPEAKER_00]: But not according to this sutta, obviously not according to the other suttas, but anyway, just if you look at this sutta, you realize that

77
00:07:08,608 --> 00:07:26,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Having developed jhāna rightly, being withdrawn from sensuality, being withdrawn from hindrances, with thinking and pondering, pleasure and joy and borderless seclusion, that is the result of the right strivings, of the right efforts, of the right surmountings, of the right contemplations.

78
00:07:29,250 --> 00:07:37,734
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why first jhāna, second jhāna, third jhāna, fourth jhāna... Not that, oh, they're irrelevant or they're pointless or secondary.

79
00:07:37,754 --> 00:07:37,915
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

80
00:07:37,995 --> 00:07:43,517
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just, they're pretty much already types of a fruit of the right effort.

81
00:07:44,258 --> 00:07:53,803
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if a person is rightly contemplating the danger in sensuality, as that Anguttara Nikaya, early one, says, I can't remember the number, says basically that one can enter jhāna having not

82
00:07:54,818 --> 00:07:55,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Understood.

83
00:07:55,658 --> 00:07:59,340
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger, gratification and the escape in regards to sensuality, that's not possible.

84
00:08:01,001 --> 00:08:02,422
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could see that's the work.

85
00:08:02,842 --> 00:08:06,023
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the wearing away.

86
00:08:06,103 --> 00:08:06,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Diligence.

87
00:08:06,904 --> 00:08:09,925
[SPEAKER_00]: Diligent contemplation of the hindrances, unwholesome states.

88
00:08:10,846 --> 00:08:11,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Not abstractly.

89
00:08:12,286 --> 00:08:17,009
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where this second part, the exposition of wearing away, kind of hammers it in.

90
00:08:17,129 --> 00:08:22,952
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like theoretical spreading of loving kindness and wishing well everybody and so on.

91
00:08:22,972 --> 00:08:23,172
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

92
00:08:23,192 --> 00:08:23,452
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like

93
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:43,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Literal daily choices that you make by body, speech and mind that pertain to cruelty, killing, stealing, not being celibate, lying, speaking divisively, harshly, frivolously, harboring covetousness, ill-will mentally.

94
00:08:43,435 --> 00:08:45,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are choices that you are accepting.

95
00:08:46,057 --> 00:08:51,742
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mind, as I say often, can come up with these things and you can't prevent that.

96
00:08:52,930 --> 00:09:01,233
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can certainly prevent adding more fuel to that fire through your own non-diligent, ignorant, wrongly emphasized choices.

97
00:09:02,314 --> 00:09:03,194
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's kind of the point.

98
00:09:03,554 --> 00:09:04,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will be cruel.

99
00:09:04,735 --> 00:09:07,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the whole world can be cruel.

100
00:09:08,516 --> 00:09:14,939
[SPEAKER_00]: The whole world can find justifications for killing, stealing, lying, speaking divisively.

101
00:09:16,379 --> 00:09:16,880
[SPEAKER_00]: I won't do it.

102
00:09:18,520 --> 00:09:19,761
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter the reason.

103
00:09:20,261 --> 00:09:20,801
[SPEAKER_00]: I won't do it.

104
00:09:21,141 --> 00:09:22,422
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I won't choose it.

105
00:09:23,621 --> 00:09:25,122
[SPEAKER_00]: I will be presented with an option.

106
00:09:25,743 --> 00:09:37,852
[SPEAKER_00]: I could be cruel, I could be harsh, I could be rude, I could be disrespectful, I could be killing, stealing, I could not be celibate, but I won't do it.

107
00:09:38,833 --> 00:09:39,694
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the difference.

108
00:09:41,835 --> 00:09:46,259
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the training, as we often say, as the suttas repeat left and right.

109
00:09:48,855 --> 00:09:52,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Exercising the right choices towards skillful by body, speech, and mind.

110
00:09:53,017 --> 00:10:04,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just adopting them, because the authority told you so, but because you are actually wanting to train your citta through those choices, i.e.

111
00:10:04,582 --> 00:10:07,383
[SPEAKER_00]: those actions, or abstaining from certain actions.

112
00:10:08,344 --> 00:10:10,225
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like... same like that other sutta.

113
00:10:10,245 --> 00:10:16,308
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it may be in Majjhima as well, where it was those different arahants, when they listed

114
00:10:18,553 --> 00:10:30,370
[SPEAKER_00]: When they were asked, like, what's the most amazing thing, what's the single thing that everybody should develop, and then they kind of listed their abilities, they were kind of... that they developed in the process of arahantship.

115
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:53,896
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha came, and then they asked him, and he says, yeah, whatever, all of you, which is, you know, he was a jhāna master, he was this, he was that, he was the Prabhupāda Mahārāja, he says, yeah, yeah, all that's great, but actually, the most correct answer, that one factor, that is the most important, was none of those special powers and results and liberations, it was the diligence.

116
00:10:54,797 --> 00:11:01,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it was the diligence as a factor that made them individually develop all those amazing things.

117
00:11:02,923 --> 00:11:06,045
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is kind of the same, like you think, like, oh, first jhāna, second jhāna.

118
00:11:06,065 --> 00:11:12,029
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, that's the correct result of correct practice.

119
00:11:12,970 --> 00:11:15,812
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by all means, it should be strived for.

120
00:11:15,832 --> 00:11:20,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you should, by all means, make the effort to withdraw your mind from hindrances and sensuality.

121
00:11:20,555 --> 00:11:23,417
[SPEAKER_00]: To remove the weight of sensual desire from your citta.

122
00:11:25,199 --> 00:11:26,060
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the work.

123
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:41,276
[SPEAKER_00]: But you could see, like, if somebody develops that and then abides in a state that, basically, citta is not touched by debt, prison, danger, all those similes that the first jhāna described, one can think, like, oh, wow, this is that purification.

124
00:11:41,356 --> 00:11:44,839
[SPEAKER_00]: This is that... this is what's bringing me to arahantship.

125
00:11:45,788 --> 00:11:47,650
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said, well, actually, no.

126
00:11:47,710 --> 00:11:50,652
[SPEAKER_00]: It was the work that you did beforehand that led you to that point.

127
00:11:51,293 --> 00:12:04,285
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, in that another sutta, he actually says, like, it was a sutta that says that, basically, arahantship can be attained on the basis of first jhāna, second jhāna, third jhāna, fourth jhāna, which is kind of used to

128
00:12:05,747 --> 00:12:11,273
[SPEAKER_00]: to show that, yes, you need to have at least the first jhāna to the extent necessary for the other hundred.

129
00:12:11,894 --> 00:12:13,316
[SPEAKER_00]: But then the Buddha described it.

130
00:12:13,336 --> 00:12:21,104
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, like, you will have a monk or something who would be... who develops the first jhāna and abides in it, and then I would tell him

131
00:12:21,965 --> 00:12:41,530
[SPEAKER_00]: After that, that, okay, now, regard everything in that first jhāna, everything that's skillful, noble, cleansed, debt-free, regard that as peril, that, that, as everything else you regard, basically, as all the ways you regard essentiality that led you towards the first jhāna.

132
00:12:42,170 --> 00:12:45,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, continue regarding the first jhāna in that way as well.

133
00:12:45,891 --> 00:12:46,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

134
00:12:46,712 --> 00:12:55,798
[SPEAKER_00]: So that his mind completely turns away from that noble state, so to speak, because then it will be impossible to return anything lower than that.

135
00:12:57,059 --> 00:13:04,083
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's that kind of exercising the right choice, recognizing the the choice that's influenced by

136
00:13:05,159 --> 00:13:27,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Lust, aversion, distraction, obviously, all these, how many, 44 or something, all these other negative factors, but obviously, they all boil down to exercising the choice that's still, like, either rooted in ignorance, rooted in complacency, rooted in lack of diligence, rooted in lack of contemplation, or not.

137
00:13:29,378 --> 00:13:33,481
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't have to... and why this sutta is so good, it's because you don't have to wait.

138
00:13:33,961 --> 00:13:41,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I've got to wait for the first jhāna, to practice those first jhāna, then I'll start exercising all of these right practices.

139
00:13:41,107 --> 00:13:41,247
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

140
00:13:41,307 --> 00:13:44,829
[SPEAKER_00]: These right practices is what's leading you in the direction of jhāna anyway.

141
00:13:46,511 --> 00:13:56,498
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on a day-to-day basis, whenever you are presented with the choice to follow wrong intention, or to abstain from it, what do you do?

142
00:13:59,740 --> 00:14:11,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever you're presented to engage in frivolous talk, to relish it, to enjoy it, do you follow it or do you say, no, I'll abstain from it?

143
00:14:13,187 --> 00:14:22,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever the mind wants to speak behind someone's back, derive satisfaction from speaking divisively or gossiping, do you accept it?

144
00:14:22,452 --> 00:14:23,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you accept that offer?

145
00:14:23,472 --> 00:14:24,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you accept that possibility?

146
00:14:25,994 --> 00:14:26,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you say no?

147
00:14:28,427 --> 00:14:32,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you don't forget what should be recollected and where the emphasis should be.

148
00:14:33,029 --> 00:14:35,010
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the training.

149
00:14:35,030 --> 00:14:35,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Body, speech and mind.

150
00:14:37,011 --> 00:14:38,672
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it can't be any clearer.

151
00:14:38,792 --> 00:14:52,198
[SPEAKER_00]: The sutta cannot summarize it in any clearer manner than this, as to what is the training that leads to the complete purification of the mind and liberation and jhāna and everything else.

152
00:14:53,417 --> 00:15:06,162
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly why, like, see, it goes, after the Buddha goes to all this describing of what actually is wearing away, the next section is the exposition of, by intent, eye choice.

153
00:15:07,443 --> 00:15:11,344
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all of these are choices that people make.

154
00:15:11,924 --> 00:15:20,446
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why I keep saying, that's the difference between having your mind say, come up with the choice, options for you.

155
00:15:20,786 --> 00:15:24,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I could be non-celibate on this occasion.

156
00:15:24,487 --> 00:15:26,328
[SPEAKER_00]: I could say a lie on this occasion.

157
00:15:27,448 --> 00:15:28,909
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not your problem.

158
00:15:29,209 --> 00:15:30,289
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not your choice.

159
00:15:32,009 --> 00:15:34,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Your choice is, do you accept the given options?

160
00:15:38,034 --> 00:15:38,555
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

161
00:15:38,575 --> 00:15:40,338
[SPEAKER_00]: These choices lead the way for purification.

162
00:15:40,378 --> 00:15:41,179
[SPEAKER_00]: These choices don't.

163
00:15:51,416 --> 00:16:03,500
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's where the next... the next section says, Chunda, I say that even the arising of intent towards beneficial qualities is very helpful, let alone following that embodied speech.

164
00:16:04,141 --> 00:16:06,781
[SPEAKER_00]: Consequently, then, the opposite has to be true.

165
00:16:07,822 --> 00:16:16,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the arising of the intent towards unbeneficial qualities is not very helpful, let alone following those thoughts in body and speech.

166
00:16:19,040 --> 00:16:23,822
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you should give rise to following intents, following choices.

167
00:16:23,842 --> 00:16:26,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I intend to choose rightly.

168
00:16:27,504 --> 00:16:39,769
[SPEAKER_00]: When presented with cruelty, killing, stealing, all the precepts, non-celibacy, negligence, faithlessness, shamelessness, imprudence, being unlearned, I will choose the opposite.

169
00:16:40,890 --> 00:16:45,232
[SPEAKER_00]: I will maintain the intent to choose the right thing, to abstain from the pull

170
00:16:46,535 --> 00:16:51,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Of the grain, with the current of all these unskillful choices.

171
00:16:54,740 --> 00:16:56,601
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, Willie gives the example.

172
00:16:56,641 --> 00:17:03,504
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like... Chunda, suppose there was an uneven path and another even path to circumvent it.

173
00:17:04,345 --> 00:17:07,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, suppose there's an uneven ford and another even ford to circumvent it.

174
00:17:07,666 --> 00:17:12,569
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same way, a cruel individual circumvents the cruelty by not being cruel.

175
00:17:12,589 --> 00:17:13,810
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no third option.

176
00:17:14,616 --> 00:17:18,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, let me... let me do a bit of both and find my way out of it.

177
00:17:19,520 --> 00:17:26,605
[SPEAKER_00]: So, these are the binary choices when it comes to... That's why they boil down to this core of the... of the intent.

178
00:17:27,305 --> 00:17:33,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Cruelty, ill-will, sensual desire, hindrances, because there is no middle ground there.

179
00:17:33,370 --> 00:17:36,032
[SPEAKER_00]: You're either allowing the animal to control you,

180
00:17:37,127 --> 00:17:45,911
[SPEAKER_00]: Accepting the given options, pressures, stuff that's been speaking into your ear, or not, and maintaining perspective in face of it.

181
00:17:48,152 --> 00:17:52,014
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not very complex, but, yeah, that doesn't mean it's easy.

182
00:17:54,695 --> 00:18:04,340
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, an individual who is attached to his own views, holding them tight, refusing to let them go, circumvents it by not being attached to his own views, not holding tight, by letting them go easily.

183
00:18:04,380 --> 00:18:05,500
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you let go of the views?

184
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:06,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, by questioning them.

185
00:18:07,853 --> 00:18:14,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you blindly defending the standpoints and views because they provide you with a sense of safety, confidence?

186
00:18:16,539 --> 00:18:21,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are you willing, at any given moment, to question your views?

187
00:18:22,364 --> 00:18:24,786
[SPEAKER_00]: If a question presents itself, you won't shy away from it.

188
00:18:25,106 --> 00:18:31,370
[SPEAKER_00]: You will follow it through, even if it proves that you were completely wrong in the core of your being, for what you believed in.

189
00:18:32,391 --> 00:18:33,472
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you overcome your views.

190
00:18:35,866 --> 00:18:42,790
[SPEAKER_00]: So, Chunda, all unbeneficial qualities lead downwards, while all beneficial qualities lead upwards.

191
00:18:43,891 --> 00:18:47,613
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same way, a cruel individual goes upwards by not being cruel.

192
00:18:48,513 --> 00:18:54,196
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by exercising the right choices, the mind's going upwards.

193
00:18:54,577 --> 00:18:58,879
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he goes to the... the last bit.

194
00:18:58,979 --> 00:18:59,600
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like...

195
00:19:02,937 --> 00:19:10,103
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're sinking in the mud yourself, Chunda, it is quite impossible for you to pull out someone else who is also sinking in the mud.

196
00:19:11,143 --> 00:19:16,347
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're not sinking in the mud yourself, it is quite possible for you to pull out someone else who is sinking in the mud.

197
00:19:17,328 --> 00:19:25,274
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you are not tamed, trained, quenched yourself, it is quite impossible for you to help tame, train and quench someone else.

198
00:19:26,995 --> 00:19:31,039
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you are tamed, trained and quenched yourself, it is quite possible for you to tame

199
00:19:31,922 --> 00:19:33,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Train and quench somebody else.

200
00:19:35,545 --> 00:19:37,748
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, kind of common sense, you would think.

201
00:19:39,749 --> 00:19:43,853
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the same way, a cruel individual quenches by not being cruel.

202
00:19:44,694 --> 00:19:55,104
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one quenches the mind, trains the mind, develops the right view, has the mind going upwards,

203
00:19:57,578 --> 00:20:01,200
[SPEAKER_00]: By exercising... by maintaining the right intent, i.e.

204
00:20:01,280 --> 00:20:08,723
[SPEAKER_00]: the right context, and by exercising the right choices on the basis of that intent, which, see, intent is already a choice.

205
00:20:09,003 --> 00:20:11,004
[SPEAKER_00]: You choose to ignore the context or not.

206
00:20:11,884 --> 00:20:23,049
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like a more general choice, on account of which you will be then exercising more specific choices that are right, that are going... circumventing the bad ones.

207
00:20:28,554 --> 00:20:38,158
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it says, so, Chunda, I've told the expositions of wearing away, arousing intent, the way around, going up and quenching.

208
00:20:39,079 --> 00:20:48,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of compassion, I've done what teachers should do, who wants... what a teacher should do, who wants what is best for his disciples.

209
00:20:48,683 --> 00:20:54,386
[SPEAKER_00]: Here are the roots of trees, here the empty huts, develop jhāna, Chunda, do not be negligent, do not regret it later, this is my instruction.

210
00:20:55,473 --> 00:20:56,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, by the way, this part developed jhāna.

211
00:20:57,415 --> 00:20:59,697
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like jhāna is just used as a verb, really.

212
00:21:00,037 --> 00:21:02,059
[SPEAKER_00]: It just means contemplate.

213
00:21:02,339 --> 00:21:03,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Contemplate, chunda, don't be negligent.

214
00:21:03,981 --> 00:21:07,804
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean enter the first jhāna or something.

215
00:21:08,685 --> 00:21:21,316
[SPEAKER_00]: If you practice contemplation rightly, correctly, surmount the unwholesome things, withdraw them, withdraw your mind from them sufficiently, you will enter the first comprehension, as I like to call it.

216
00:21:24,493 --> 00:21:26,134
[SPEAKER_00]: On account of the wearing away.

217
00:21:26,374 --> 00:21:28,715
[SPEAKER_00]: On account of the right practice.

218
00:21:29,595 --> 00:21:31,676
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, yeah, you know what to do, rest.

219
00:21:32,777 --> 00:21:38,379
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then it says, this is what the auspicious one said, pleased, venerable Mahārāja Chunda delighted in what the Buddha said.

220
00:21:39,520 --> 00:21:44,082
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, 44 items have been stated, organized into five sections.

221
00:21:44,662 --> 00:21:48,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Wearing away is the name of this discourse, which is deep as the ocean.

222
00:21:48,944 --> 00:21:51,025
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of the core, pretty much.

223
00:21:52,139 --> 00:21:55,138
[SPEAKER_00]: It just ties into, like, even... because, you know, you had, like, you had those...

224
00:21:57,030 --> 00:22:11,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Complex... well... say, more kind of advanced, higher types of instructions, detailed expositions on patitsaṁpāda, this or that, which, you know, would go over people's heads, generally.

225
00:22:12,361 --> 00:22:17,164
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you had those instructions given to some monks and nuns.

226
00:22:17,264 --> 00:22:20,607
[SPEAKER_00]: Go to seclusion and anything that you know is unbeneficial, don't cultivate it.

227
00:22:20,647 --> 00:22:22,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything you know is beneficial, cultivate it.

228
00:22:22,408 --> 00:22:24,309
[SPEAKER_00]: They did that for three months, came back, arahants.

229
00:22:25,484 --> 00:22:38,833
[SPEAKER_00]: So, those kind of polar opposites on two different, like, ends of, well, same spectrum of right instruction, but very two different, like, very far apart, resulted in arahantship.

230
00:22:39,874 --> 00:22:46,438
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the detailed advanced expositions resulted in arahantship for some, and then just abandoned the unbeneficial qualities.

231
00:22:46,939 --> 00:22:48,880
[SPEAKER_00]: That resulted in arahantship for another.

232
00:22:50,801 --> 00:22:53,223
[SPEAKER_00]: And this kind of sutta shows you how.

233
00:22:55,812 --> 00:23:09,318
[SPEAKER_00]: It shows you what's in common to both, which is basically that core principle of exercising the skillful choice at the expense of the unskillful one by body, speech and mind.

234
00:23:10,719 --> 00:23:12,839
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to be aware of the choice.

235
00:23:12,920 --> 00:23:21,103
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I keep saying, like, yes, you can take on the precepts because the authority told you so and it can help you in the beginning, but if it's kept at the arm's length,

236
00:23:24,587 --> 00:23:33,136
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's kept as a duty that I must blindly adhere to and never allow my mind to, you know, think about and maybe highlight that there is always a choice.

237
00:23:33,356 --> 00:23:36,659
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, at any given point you can get up and not be a monk anymore.

238
00:23:37,620 --> 00:23:39,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Does that choice scare you, thinking about it?

239
00:23:40,283 --> 00:23:43,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Does the mind get wild when that's... when that option is presented?

240
00:23:43,546 --> 00:23:45,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you are not training anything.

241
00:23:45,788 --> 00:23:47,550
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just keeping it down, covered up.

242
00:23:49,498 --> 00:23:57,884
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's not just enough to memorize these 44 items and then just automatically always choose right.

243
00:23:58,805 --> 00:24:00,366
[SPEAKER_00]: The choice needs to be felt.

244
00:24:01,607 --> 00:24:02,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Needs to be...

245
00:24:02,928 --> 00:24:04,630
[SPEAKER_00]: It's something you need to be conscious of.

246
00:24:04,830 --> 00:24:07,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, I could actually...

247
00:24:07,192 --> 00:24:07,752
[SPEAKER_00]: It's 50-50.

248
00:24:08,392 --> 00:24:10,854
[SPEAKER_00]: I could get up and leave, but I choose not to.

249
00:24:14,437 --> 00:24:18,080
[SPEAKER_00]: I could say something unwholesome, but I choose not to.

250
00:24:19,922 --> 00:24:32,408
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the missing kind of aspect, angle, in people's practice, in people who keep the precepts and do everything rightly, but still don't have the right view.

251
00:24:33,529 --> 00:24:36,590
[SPEAKER_00]: It means they're doing it through Sīlabhātparamāsa.

252
00:24:36,610 --> 00:24:38,091
[SPEAKER_00]: They're doing it at the arm's length.

253
00:24:41,713 --> 00:24:43,614
[SPEAKER_00]: They're doing it kind of

254
00:24:48,044 --> 00:24:58,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, through at least a degree of avoiding of responsibility for that core choice, you could stop doing it at any given moment.

255
00:24:58,608 --> 00:25:03,869
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, you can use the environment, use tradition, use the learning, use all of that.

256
00:25:04,470 --> 00:25:17,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, I'll take on the precepts in that sense, but not to shy away or mask the choice that you have every day to maintain it or abandon it, to practice or not, but to actually be able to come closer to it

257
00:25:18,348 --> 00:25:24,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Without your mind overwhelming you in a kind of excitement towards the prospect of letting it loose.

258
00:25:26,995 --> 00:25:31,699
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's that level, the right level of endurance and the right level of choice.

259
00:25:32,540 --> 00:25:34,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, obviously, you have to be patient about.

260
00:25:34,441 --> 00:25:38,925
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just decide the right level where you're gonna decide the right things.

261
00:25:39,726 --> 00:25:40,767
[SPEAKER_00]: It needs to be discerned.

262
00:25:41,430 --> 00:25:44,652
[SPEAKER_00]: When I said what do all these things converge upon, they converge upon the choice.

263
00:25:45,253 --> 00:25:46,954
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what's in common to all those things.

264
00:25:46,994 --> 00:25:48,795
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the fact that it's your choice.

265
00:25:49,676 --> 00:25:50,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Others may do it like this.

266
00:25:51,738 --> 00:25:52,638
[SPEAKER_00]: We won't do it here.

267
00:25:53,899 --> 00:25:57,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, almost like we, as in like me and my own mind.

268
00:25:57,102 --> 00:26:02,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Hey, look, two of us, we're not doing this.

269
00:26:02,906 --> 00:26:03,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will be non-celibate.

270
00:26:04,227 --> 00:26:04,827
[SPEAKER_00]: We won't be.

271
00:26:06,068 --> 00:26:07,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Others will be negligent.

272
00:26:07,409 --> 00:26:08,250
[SPEAKER_00]: We won't do that.

273
00:26:10,020 --> 00:26:13,121
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm choosing for both of us, so to speak.

274
00:26:14,961 --> 00:26:19,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Ajahn, does this mean that the Buddha answered Venerable Chöndor's question if the fundamentals are enough?

275
00:26:19,523 --> 00:26:20,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, but you need to drill them in.

276
00:26:21,303 --> 00:26:23,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Or no, there is more to it.

277
00:26:24,564 --> 00:26:27,985
[SPEAKER_00]: I, not just purification of the body and speech, but by mind as well.

278
00:26:28,605 --> 00:26:29,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, yeah.

279
00:26:29,986 --> 00:26:30,326
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like

280
00:26:34,160 --> 00:26:45,390
[SPEAKER_00]: The views are not gonna automatically fall off just if somebody's, as I say, virtuous, keeping the precepts and so on.

281
00:26:46,871 --> 00:26:56,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the effort needs to be made with, like, right contemplation, diligence, in regard to hindrances, in regard to all the, you know, unwholesome choices that carry the roots of

282
00:26:58,518 --> 00:27:00,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Greed, aversion, distraction.

283
00:27:00,319 --> 00:27:05,222
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, like, well, there is more to it, yes, but not like more to it you do something else.

284
00:27:05,403 --> 00:27:16,330
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just, there is more of the same thing, but it needs to be done, like, on the mental level, well, without any tolerance for exceptions.

285
00:27:18,671 --> 00:27:20,032
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's gradual.

286
00:27:20,512 --> 00:27:24,315
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you will not just magically be able to, like, keep your mind above sensual desire.

287
00:27:25,208 --> 00:27:26,591
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, so you start physically.

288
00:27:26,631 --> 00:27:28,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, keep your body out of the sensual desire.

289
00:27:28,474 --> 00:27:29,336
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start verbally.

290
00:27:29,396 --> 00:27:30,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Add verbal aspect.

291
00:27:30,558 --> 00:27:31,700
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, well, don't talk about it.

292
00:27:31,720 --> 00:27:32,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't be, like, spreading gossip.

293
00:27:32,963 --> 00:27:36,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't be engaging in all these other compensating practices.

294
00:27:37,543 --> 00:27:45,228
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, comes to a point, it's like, oh, yeah, I can see that mental choice now, because I haven't been, obviously, muddying the water through, like, coarse physical or verbal actions.

295
00:27:45,989 --> 00:27:51,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, the choice of those thoughts enduring in your mind is presented, and it's like, oh, I don't have to pick them up, actually.

296
00:27:51,753 --> 00:27:55,375
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, I don't have to pick things up with my hand, when they're in front of me.

297
00:27:55,435 --> 00:27:56,476
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to destroy them.

298
00:27:56,936 --> 00:27:58,097
[SPEAKER_00]: I just need to not pick it up.

299
00:27:59,578 --> 00:28:01,700
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, I see the same principle mentally.

300
00:28:02,737 --> 00:28:03,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoughts have arisen.

301
00:28:03,758 --> 00:28:04,758
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to pick them up.

302
00:28:04,978 --> 00:28:05,899
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to get rid of them.

303
00:28:06,019 --> 00:28:07,060
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to do anything about them.

304
00:28:07,200 --> 00:28:10,302
[SPEAKER_00]: I just don't have to pick them up and I'm free from them.

305
00:28:11,102 --> 00:28:18,787
[SPEAKER_00]: But in order for that mental choice to become apparent, yeah, the the right physical and verbal choices need to be cultivated, well, sufficiently long.

306
00:28:19,047 --> 00:28:19,688
[SPEAKER_00]: How long that is?

307
00:28:19,728 --> 00:28:20,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, person will see.

308
00:28:22,029 --> 00:28:23,450
[SPEAKER_00]: There's not like a preset time.

309
00:28:25,306 --> 00:28:27,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Ajahn, this might be related to the last question.

310
00:28:27,969 --> 00:28:33,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Does the first part of the sutta suggest that people can get to the eighth jhāna without right view?

311
00:28:33,836 --> 00:28:38,180
[SPEAKER_00]: You mean like the four immaterial attainment?

312
00:28:39,341 --> 00:28:40,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yes, other suttas do say that.

313
00:28:41,984 --> 00:28:46,869
[SPEAKER_00]: A person can withdraw their mind rightly.

314
00:28:48,090 --> 00:28:57,818
[SPEAKER_00]: And develop 1st, 2nd, 3rd, 4th jhāna and then the subsequent immaterial establishments of the mind.

315
00:28:59,880 --> 00:29:10,069
[SPEAKER_00]: But, again, even those people without the right view would be walking along the right lines.

316
00:29:11,696 --> 00:29:26,724
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they might not technically have had enough right instruction to see through their conceit and the sense of I am in that subtle way, but the only way to enter the first jhāna or go further is through abandoning sensuality.

317
00:29:27,224 --> 00:29:33,848
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like anāgāmī, like... but withdrawing your mind, completely drying it from sensuality internally.

318
00:29:35,658 --> 00:29:46,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Overcoming the five hindrances, again, not like an arahant permanently, but at least to the extent that you've been diligently practicing the way out of those things through understanding them.

319
00:29:47,761 --> 00:29:51,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Not through, like, self-hypnotizing or something, where you are unaware of those things.

320
00:29:53,342 --> 00:30:04,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, how can you... how can you be exercising right choices, as this sutta describes and says this is what wearing away actually is, that even jhāna is the result of,

321
00:30:05,509 --> 00:30:28,394
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are hypnotized, if you are unaware of the options, if you are completely oblivious to the context, to the intent, to what to choose, what's presented there, what mind leans towards... That's why any, that's what I keep saying, like any types of self-hypnosis or practice is just taking you away from where the actual work is done.

322
00:30:29,494 --> 00:30:32,135
[SPEAKER_00]: And see, even the ones that have entered in the first jhāna,

323
00:30:33,128 --> 00:30:41,175
[SPEAKER_00]: According to all the suttas, with thinking and pondering, enjoy born of withdrawal from the unwholesome states.

324
00:30:41,355 --> 00:30:48,061
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the knowledge that you are safe from the threat, safe from death, safe from prison, safe from danger.

325
00:30:50,243 --> 00:30:52,905
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order to know that you are safe from the danger,

326
00:30:53,958 --> 00:31:05,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the only way to know... the only way to be safe from the danger and feel that safety is to fully understand the extent of danger, not to pretend that it's not there because you focused on your kneecaps or something.

327
00:31:06,006 --> 00:31:06,367
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

328
00:31:08,828 --> 00:31:15,033
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, people can technically get jhānas without the right view, but, again, today the only actual

329
00:31:17,815 --> 00:31:29,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowledge of jhānas, yes, okay, like putting aside the contemporary later interpretations and stuff like that, but sooner or later, people would basically fall back onto the suttas, because that's where the idea of jhāna comes from.

330
00:31:29,862 --> 00:31:43,891
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that somebody can actually practice the jhānas rightly, for the jhānas rightly, and attain the jhānas rightly, without having understood the importance, the emphasis, the training aspect of citta, through these right choices, that's not possible.

331
00:31:45,303 --> 00:31:51,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Technically, it's possible that somebody was doing it back in the day, because there were lots of not-as-wrong instructions.

332
00:31:52,485 --> 00:31:59,346
[SPEAKER_00]: But today, that somebody can arrive at that, without having come across the right instruction of the Buddha and the suttas, it's highly unlikely, let's say like that.

333
00:32:00,246 --> 00:32:11,709
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if somebody were to develop the first jhāna or higher, it has to be developed through the exercising of the same right choices, same withdrawal from the same unwholesome things.

334
00:32:11,789 --> 00:32:12,029
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

335
00:32:13,142 --> 00:32:14,723
[SPEAKER_00]: They'll almost certainly get the right view.

336
00:32:15,563 --> 00:32:17,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Even before their jhāna, pretty much.

337
00:32:21,906 --> 00:32:28,789
[SPEAKER_00]: The next question... Ajahn, there is an intellectual understanding of the principles.

338
00:32:29,549 --> 00:32:34,271
[SPEAKER_00]: However, sometimes, due to undeveloped mindfulness, the choices seem to have been made for you on some sense.

339
00:32:35,011 --> 00:32:38,333
[SPEAKER_00]: What can we do to develop the right... How can the choices be made for you?

340
00:32:41,475 --> 00:32:42,876
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not possible.

341
00:32:42,916 --> 00:32:44,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Choice cannot be made for you.

342
00:32:44,777 --> 00:32:48,099
[SPEAKER_00]: You can certainly have very weighty options.

343
00:32:49,419 --> 00:32:52,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Some options will be pressing you and threatening you more than others.

344
00:32:53,061 --> 00:32:56,343
[SPEAKER_00]: But no choice can be made for you.

345
00:32:56,403 --> 00:32:56,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Ever.

346
00:32:59,485 --> 00:33:00,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it's not a choice.

347
00:33:02,686 --> 00:33:05,068
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what people are afraid of, basically.

348
00:33:05,984 --> 00:33:18,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you start approaching everything through the recognition of your choices, it can get very scary very quickly, because you realize, well, nothing obliges you to live or die.

349
00:33:20,567 --> 00:33:33,329
[SPEAKER_00]: No external criteria, no external reason, no external... You choose them to... you choose those things as reasons, but you could then also choose them to stop being reasons, basically, for whatever you're doing or not doing.

350
00:33:34,302 --> 00:33:45,151
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, like, in existential philosophy, Sartre was referring to it, I remember, as a... once a person takes on the kind of existential responsibility, i.e.

351
00:33:45,831 --> 00:33:54,298
[SPEAKER_00]: start taking responsibility for the choices and recognize that there is never... that it's actually impossible for choice to be made for you.

352
00:33:54,978 --> 00:33:56,340
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the one accepting it or not.

353
00:33:57,000 --> 00:33:59,002
[SPEAKER_00]: He called it as monstrous freedom.

354
00:34:00,012 --> 00:34:06,495
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, when you recognize how free you actually are, it can be overwhelmingly frightful, scary.

355
00:34:07,435 --> 00:34:13,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, at any given moment, anybody can just get up and walk out from anything.

356
00:34:16,720 --> 00:34:17,780
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, that's scary.

357
00:34:21,642 --> 00:34:23,343
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's something you have to get used to.

358
00:34:24,083 --> 00:34:24,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Like,

359
00:34:25,655 --> 00:34:30,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Getting closer to the recognition that choice always rests with you, mentally.

360
00:34:31,758 --> 00:34:42,723
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter the options, no matter the weight of those options, no matter the repercussions, no matter the consequences, choice cannot be made for you.

361
00:34:42,783 --> 00:34:52,427
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, again, something I remember, like, it gets casually chanted, like, in the monasteries and stuff, like, oh, we are the owners of our karma, and it's like,

362
00:34:53,735 --> 00:34:55,216
[SPEAKER_00]: People don't realize the extent of that.

363
00:34:55,796 --> 00:34:57,638
[SPEAKER_00]: They think like, oh, yeah, we are the owners of...

364
00:34:57,658 --> 00:34:59,879
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, karma is choice.

365
00:35:00,079 --> 00:35:01,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Every action is intention.

366
00:35:02,781 --> 00:35:04,622
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no action without the choice.

367
00:35:04,702 --> 00:35:10,866
[SPEAKER_00]: So, choice, it's already something that pretty much sets and determines your karma, just as the sutra says.

368
00:35:10,926 --> 00:35:14,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, just the rising of beneficial or unbeneficial intent.

369
00:35:15,530 --> 00:35:16,050
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already.

370
00:35:19,062 --> 00:35:25,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Taking you up or taking you down, let alone following through with body and speech and actions.

371
00:35:27,006 --> 00:35:34,770
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the common casual chant, well, we are the owners of our actions, like, yeah, through and through, all the way.

372
00:35:36,190 --> 00:35:43,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Your action to choose to... your choice to choose to get up and continue living your life the way you live your life, that's your karma.

373
00:35:46,017 --> 00:35:49,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Your choice to change, to do something about it, well, that's your karma.

374
00:35:50,882 --> 00:35:53,664
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, oh, do you recognize how far that goes?

375
00:35:55,105 --> 00:35:55,426
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing.

376
00:35:55,966 --> 00:35:58,909
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing that can come in between you and that freedom.

377
00:36:00,722 --> 00:36:08,564
[SPEAKER_00]: And the sooner you recognize that, the sooner you admit it, more importantly, well, the sooner can citta stop being afraid from it, of it.

378
00:36:09,024 --> 00:36:10,144
[SPEAKER_00]: It will get used to it.

379
00:36:10,324 --> 00:36:13,925
[SPEAKER_00]: It can be overwhelming in the beginning, but that's why you have the precepts.

380
00:36:14,065 --> 00:36:22,347
[SPEAKER_00]: They're preventing you from spiraling into the unwanted directions, on account of the same freedom of choice.

381
00:36:23,928 --> 00:36:26,789
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the mind can settle in that, can get used to that.

382
00:36:27,569 --> 00:36:28,629
[SPEAKER_00]: And it won't be as frightening.

383
00:36:29,596 --> 00:36:30,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not a surprise anymore.

384
00:36:31,677 --> 00:36:34,519
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the training becomes elevated.

385
00:36:34,539 --> 00:36:58,614
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, now you can... you can get closer to that edge of freedom and really purify it, really develop the right intent, maintain that right intent, right context, and then all the... all the presented options to choose these 44 unwholesome things or 44 opposites.

386
00:36:59,960 --> 00:37:01,080
[SPEAKER_00]: You will know what to choose.

387
00:37:01,781 --> 00:37:03,201
[SPEAKER_00]: You will know what to emphasize.

388
00:37:03,261 --> 00:37:06,822
[SPEAKER_00]: You will know what not to, like, tolerate, to give in and add more fuel to it.

389
00:37:09,063 --> 00:37:15,605
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the same instruction, same principle they would have started from the beginning, like in the, say, early stages of training.

390
00:37:16,246 --> 00:37:19,647
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is already now the higher refinement of your citta.

391
00:37:20,087 --> 00:37:20,687
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same thing.

392
00:37:20,707 --> 00:37:26,049
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, as I said, in some of the suttas, that's all people needed to become arahants.

393
00:37:27,369 --> 00:37:29,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the same principle goes all the way through.

394
00:37:33,350 --> 00:37:40,235
[SPEAKER_00]: So, regarding this last question, you could see that due to the undeveloped mindfulness, the choices seem to have been made for you.

395
00:37:41,076 --> 00:37:44,018
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you chose lack of mindfulness.

396
00:37:45,119 --> 00:37:47,501
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why subsequent choices felt automatic.

397
00:37:49,362 --> 00:38:02,832
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you didn't choose the lack of mindfulness, abandonment of the context, willingly, those choices would have remained seen, not just submitted to.

398
00:38:04,902 --> 00:38:11,926
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, nothing, nothing can come between you and your responsibility for everything you choose, big or small.

399
00:38:18,870 --> 00:38:27,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Ajahn, I think you mentioned before that one usually tries to get rid of one hindrance through another hindrance, and that's how one ends up accepting some hindrance.

400
00:38:28,216 --> 00:38:29,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Could you speak more about this?

401
00:38:32,928 --> 00:38:35,829
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't quite remember what was the context of that, but...

402
00:38:38,791 --> 00:38:49,054
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I just meant, basically, a person is just acting out of hindrances, because they're trying to get rid of the entire options presented.

403
00:38:49,975 --> 00:38:51,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's put it that way in the context of today's talk.

404
00:38:52,936 --> 00:39:01,099
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the problem with hindrances... For example, maybe there's a version, you have a version to another version, and try to get rid of it.

405
00:39:03,165 --> 00:39:12,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can have aversion to your aversion, but... you might not be able to help that, but it's like, do you go about it the right way or the wrong way?

406
00:39:14,394 --> 00:39:17,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you trying to get rid of the choices of aversion, i.e.

407
00:39:17,736 --> 00:39:19,858
[SPEAKER_00]: your citta, that's affected with aversion?

408
00:39:21,118 --> 00:39:23,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, are you recognizing that that's the original state of things?

409
00:39:24,341 --> 00:39:34,769
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Sutta says, he knows the mind of anger as mind of anger, mind of lust as mind of lust, not he tries to change it, and wrestle it, and tackle it, and get rid of it, and tailor it to his liking.

410
00:39:34,789 --> 00:39:34,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

411
00:39:36,010 --> 00:39:36,170
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

412
00:39:36,550 --> 00:39:39,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowledge is the way you tailor it to your liking, knowing it for what it is.

413
00:39:41,187 --> 00:39:42,567
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have a mind of aversion.

414
00:39:43,088 --> 00:39:50,350
[SPEAKER_00]: You know that the choices to speak, to say something, to do something on account of this aversion, are bound.

415
00:39:50,430 --> 00:39:52,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Those options are your state of mind.

416
00:39:54,871 --> 00:39:56,632
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not trying to get rid of that.

417
00:39:59,739 --> 00:40:04,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead, you are choosing to not accept the aversion while it's there.

418
00:40:04,841 --> 00:40:05,962
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the subtle difference.

419
00:40:06,882 --> 00:40:08,523
[SPEAKER_00]: And why is that difference important?

420
00:40:08,543 --> 00:40:09,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's where your choice is.

421
00:40:10,364 --> 00:40:18,267
[SPEAKER_00]: See, it would be easier if I could just remove the tempting things, including my mind states, so I don't have to feel the edge of the choice.

422
00:40:20,468 --> 00:40:28,952
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's why people fail to free themselves from hindrances and just end up from one falling into the other or going in circles, basically, and then

423
00:40:30,060 --> 00:40:41,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Developing a view that pretty much self-induced oblivion of hypnosis and half-asleep state of concentrations is the only possible peace they can know.

424
00:40:42,724 --> 00:40:45,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Because either mind bothers you or you're not aware of it.

425
00:40:46,605 --> 00:40:47,585
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no third option.

426
00:40:49,385 --> 00:40:56,307
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why, again, others will be cruel, others will be non-self, others will follow sensuality.

427
00:40:56,367 --> 00:40:58,548
[SPEAKER_00]: All of these are listed things in this sutta.

428
00:40:59,670 --> 00:41:04,114
[SPEAKER_00]: There are options that citta will come up with, through interaction with the senses and so on.

429
00:41:04,514 --> 00:41:06,456
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, but I will choose not to.

430
00:41:09,338 --> 00:41:11,139
[SPEAKER_00]: I will not add fuel.

431
00:41:11,259 --> 00:41:19,846
[SPEAKER_00]: I will not add further choices of this that would then kind of start adding towards the actions by body and speech and so on.

432
00:41:20,847 --> 00:41:23,629
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how long the possibility for cruelty remains.

433
00:41:24,850 --> 00:41:27,833
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how long the possibility for non-celibacy remains.

434
00:41:29,535 --> 00:41:30,696
[SPEAKER_00]: For negligence remains.

435
00:41:31,837 --> 00:41:32,817
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not up to you.

436
00:41:33,518 --> 00:41:36,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Up to you is to no exceptions.

437
00:41:36,520 --> 00:41:38,301
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't accept it and you don't fuel it.

438
00:41:39,582 --> 00:41:40,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the difference.

439
00:41:41,263 --> 00:41:48,367
[SPEAKER_00]: When I say one tries to get rid of the hindrance, means one tries to get rid of the entire experience as a whole, the mind.

440
00:41:48,407 --> 00:41:49,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is unpleasant.

441
00:41:49,728 --> 00:41:50,529
[SPEAKER_00]: This is unwholesome.

442
00:41:50,549 --> 00:41:51,309
[SPEAKER_00]: I read all the suttas.

443
00:41:51,329 --> 00:41:52,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Buddha said it's unwholesome.

444
00:41:52,510 --> 00:41:53,291
[SPEAKER_00]: I must get rid of it.

445
00:41:53,971 --> 00:41:55,872
[SPEAKER_00]: I must make this whole thing go away.

446
00:41:57,753 --> 00:41:58,274
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh...

447
00:41:59,748 --> 00:42:11,534
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, usually, those... those... those suttas where the Buddha talks about, like this... somebody who already sees their citta, somebody who's already saw the pañña, you know, like that... the five ways of stilling your thoughts.

448
00:42:11,614 --> 00:42:14,135
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like the Abhi citta training.

449
00:42:14,735 --> 00:42:20,378
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's somebody who's already been exercising the right choices and used things on right level, recognizes citta, the animal, for what it is.

450
00:42:21,198 --> 00:42:24,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, he can refine it in those other five ways.

451
00:42:25,830 --> 00:42:34,200
[SPEAKER_00]: But, without seeing your citta, those five ways just become five excuses to try to get rid of the things, because they bother you and you're already averse towards the whole experience.

